Programming in JavaScript<P>
<HR>
<H1>Appendix B - RISC OS programming reference</H1>
<HR>
This appendix lists the parts of the desktop's programming interface that the book uses, and a few more that
are useful. The interface is modelled on the real RISC OS window manager (the <I>Wimp</I>), but simplified
for JavaScript. The chapter that explains each part is given in brackets.<P>

<A NAME=S1></A>
<H2>Running programs</H2>
A JavaScript program is a file of type <B>JSScript</B> (&amp;F81). Double-clicking one runs it with the
command <CODE>*JSRun</CODE>, which you can also type yourself, at the command line (F12) or in a task window
(Ctrl-F12):<P>
<PRE>
*JSRun &lt;filename&gt; [&lt;parameters&gt;]
</PRE>
Each program runs as a separate <I>task</I>, named after its file, which appears in the Task Manager's
window. A program has finished when its code has run and it has nothing left that could run more of it: no
windows, icon bar icons or timers. If it printed anything, its output window stays open, with
<I>(finished)</I> in its title, until you close it.<P>
There are two kinds of program:<P>
<DL>
<DT>Scripts (chapters 1 to 10, and WordCount in chapter 12)
<DD>A file that doesn't start any line with <CODE>import</CODE> or <CODE>export</CODE>. It runs from top to
bottom, with everything in the next section ready to use.<P>
<DT>Modules (chapter 11)
<DD>A file that does. It imports what it needs and runs by calling its start function:
<PRE>
import { print, Menu } from 'riscos';
import { drawBoard } from './Board';
export default function start(task, ctx) { ... }
</PRE>
<CODE>'riscos'</CODE> gives the same names as a script has ready to use (except <CODE>task</CODE> and
<CODE>ctx</CODE>, which are passed to <CODE>start</CODE>). An import starting <CODE>./</CODE> is another file
in the same directory (<CODE>../</CODE> is the parent directory): <CODE>'./Board'</CODE> is the file
<CODE>Board</CODE>, and <CODE>'./Board.js'</CODE> is <CODE>Board/js</CODE> if there is one, otherwise
<CODE>Board</CODE>.<P>
</DL>
<H3>Applications (chapter 13)</H3>
An application is a directory whose name starts with <CODE>!</CODE>. Double-clicking it runs its
<CODE>!Run</CODE> Obey file, which usually looks like this:<P>
<PRE>
Set MyApp$Dir &lt;Obey$Dir&gt;
IconSprites &lt;MyApp$Dir&gt;.!Sprites
Run &lt;MyApp$Dir&gt;.!RunImage %*0
</PRE>
When the program is <CODE>!RunImage</CODE>, its task is named after the application. The Filer runs
<CODE>!Boot</CODE> when it first shows the application, shows the sprite named after it (in lower case), and
shows <CODE>!Help</CODE> for Help on its menu.<P>

<A NAME=S2></A>
<H2>Ready to use</H2>
<PRE>
task        this program's task
ctx         about this run:
  ctx.args    the text after the program's name (a string)
  ctx.argv    the same, split into words (an array)
  ctx.file    the program's own full file name
  ctx.dir     the directory it is in
os          the whole desktop (os.vfs, os.wimp, os.cli ...)
wimp        the window manager (menus, input focus)
vfs         the filing system: files and directories
print(a, b, ...)          show values on a line        (1)
await input(prompt)       ask for a line of text       (1)
await sleep(ms)           wait a number of ms
Menu                      make menus                   (10)
colourMenu(...)           the Wimp's colour menu       (10)
wimpColour(n)             Wimp colour n as a colour    (7)
beep()                    the warning beep             (13)
sound(ch, amp, pitch, len)   play a note               (13)
saveAs({...})             a Save box                   (12)
query({...})              a question box
infoBox(task, {...})      an "About this program" box  (10)
</PRE>
<CODE>print</CODE> shows strings as they are and other values much as they'd be written in a program, for
example <CODE>[1, 2]</CODE> or <CODE>{name: "Ann"}</CODE>. It prints in the task window the program was run
from, or else in an output window of its own, which <CODE>input</CODE> uses too.<P>

<A NAME=S3></A>
<H2>Tasks</H2>
<PRE>
task.createWindow({...})        make a window (below)        (6)
task.every(ms, fn)              call fn every ms              (9)
task.after(ms, fn)              call fn once, after ms
task.animate(fn)                call fn at every screen frame
task.addIconbarIcon({...})      an icon on the icon bar       (10)
task.onMessage('Quit', fn)      answer a message              (10)
task.reportError(text)          an error box from the program
task.quit()                     end the program               (6)
task.on('quit', fn)             fn is called when it ends
task.name                       the task's name
</PRE>
<CODE>every</CODE>, <CODE>after</CODE> and <CODE>animate</CODE> return a function that stops the timer.
Everything the task has made - windows, icons, timers - goes when it quits.<P>

<A NAME=S4></A>
<H2>Windows</H2>
<PRE>
const w = task.createWindow({
  title: 'Doodle',              the title bar text
  x: 200, y: 100,               where on the screen
  w: 400, h: 300,               its visible size
  extent: { w: 800, h: 600 },   the size of its work area
  flags: { title, close, back, toggle, size,
           vscroll, hscroll, moveable },   which furniture
  colours: { workBg: 0 },       work area colour (Wimp colour)
  workButton: 'click',          report clicks (below)
  icons: [ ... ],               icons (below)
});
</PRE>
Everything is optional. Without <CODE>flags</CODE> a window has all its furniture; with
<CODE>flags</CODE>, only the parts set to <CODE>true</CODE>. The work area is light grey (Wimp colour 1)
unless you set <CODE>workBg</CODE>. Without <CODE>workButton</CODE> the window reports no clicks in its work
area (clicks on icons with a button type are still reported).<P>
<B>Coordinates</B>: positions are in desktop pixels. On the screen, x goes right from the left edge and y
goes <I>down</I> from the top. In a work area, (0, 0) is the top left corner, and y again goes down. (Real
RISC OS measures in OS units, two to a pixel here, with y going up; this interface keeps to the way its
drawing canvas works.)<P>
<PRE>
w.open()   w.open({ behind: 'top' })   open, or bring to the front
w.close()                              close (it can be opened again)
w.delete()                             close and throw away
w.setTitle(text)                       change the title
w.setExtent({ w, h })                  change the work area size
w.isOpen                               true if open
w.useCanvas((g, rect) =&gt; { ... })      draw the work area   (7)
w.invalidate()                         ask for a redraw     (7)
w.icons[n]                             its icons
w.on(event, (ev) =&gt; { ... })           handle an event
</PRE>
<H3>Events</H3>
<PRE>
click        a mouse click: ev.button ('select', 'menu' or
             'adjust'), ev.x, ev.y (work area), ev.icon, ev.shift
doubleclick  the same, for a double click
drag         a drag started (workButton 'clickdrag')
pointermove  the pointer moved over the window: ev.x, ev.y,
             ev.buttons (1 while Select is held, 0 if none)  (8)
key          a key pressed with the input focus: ev.code, ev.char
close        the close icon was clicked (default: close it)
open         moved, resized or scrolled
dataload     files dropped on the window: ev.files[i].path,
             .name, .filetype, .size                        (12)
</PRE>
A handler returns <CODE>true</CODE> if it dealt with the event. For <CODE>close</CODE>,
<CODE>ev.preventDefault()</CODE> stops the window closing (to ask about unsaved work first, for example).<P>
<H3>workButton</H3>
<PRE>
'never'            no clicks (the default)
'click'            each click, as the button goes down
'release'          each click, as the button comes up
'doubleclick'      double clicks only
'clickdrag'        clicks, and drags
'clickdragdouble'  clicks, drags and double clicks
</PRE>

<A NAME=S5></A>
<H2>Drawing</H2>
<CODE>w.useCanvas(fn)</CODE> gives the window a canvas. Whenever it needs redrawing - when it is opened,
uncovered, scrolled, or after <CODE>w.invalidate()</CODE> - the Wimp clears it to the work area colour and
calls <CODE>fn(g, rect)</CODE>. <CODE>g</CODE> is a standard JavaScript 2D drawing context, in work area
coordinates; <CODE>rect</CODE> (<CODE>x0, y0, x1, y1</CODE>) is the part that is visible. The most useful parts
of it:<P>
<PRE>
g.fillStyle = 'red';          colour for filling (a colour name,
g.strokeStyle = '#0000ff';    '#rrggbb', or wimpColour(n))
g.lineWidth = 2;              line thickness
g.fillRect(x, y, w, h)        a filled rectangle
g.strokeRect(x, y, w, h)      an outline
g.beginPath()                 start a shape, then:
g.moveTo(x, y)  g.lineTo(x, y)   lines
g.arc(x, y, r, from, to)      a circle (angles in radians)
g.closePath()                 back to the start
g.fill()   g.stroke()         fill it, or draw its outline
g.font = 'bold 20px sans-serif';
g.textAlign = 'center';       'left', 'center' or 'right'
g.fillText(text, x, y)        text (y is the baseline)
</PRE>
<H3>Wimp colours</H3>
<PRE>
 0 white        4 grey 4       8 dark blue    12 cream
 1 grey 1       5 grey 5       9 yellow       13 dark green
 2 grey 2       6 grey 6      10 light green  14 orange
 3 grey 3       7 black       11 red          15 light blue
</PRE>

<A NAME=S6></A>
<H2>Icons</H2>
Icons in a window are given in <CODE>createWindow</CODE>'s <CODE>icons</CODE>, or added with
<CODE>w.addIcon({...})</CODE>:<P>
<PRE>
{ x: 10, y: 10, w: 100, h: 32,    position and size
  text: 'OK',                     text in it
  sprite: 'file_fff',             or a sprite
  border: true, filled: true,     outline, background
  hcentre: true,                  centre the text
  fg: 7, bg: 1,                   Wimp colours
  button: 'click',                what clicks do: 'click',
                                  'release', 'radio', 'writable'
  validation: 'R5,3',             an action button's 3D look
                                  ('R6,3' default button,
                                   'R7' a writable field)
}
w.icons[n].text                   its text
w.icons[n].setText(text)          change it
w.icons[n].setState({ selected, shaded })
</PRE>
A click on an icon gives the window a <CODE>click</CODE> event with <CODE>ev.icon</CODE> set to it.<P>

<A NAME=S7></A>
<H2>The keyboard</H2>
Keys go to the window with the <I>input focus</I>, whose title bar is cream. Give a window the focus with
<CODE>wimp.setCaret(w)</CODE>, usually when it is opened or clicked. Its <CODE>key</CODE> event has
<CODE>ev.char</CODE>, the character typed (<CODE>'a'</CODE>, <CODE>' '</CODE> ...), and
<CODE>ev.code</CODE>, the Wimp key code. Return <CODE>true</CODE> if you used the key, and nothing if not,
so that it can go on to other programs. (F12 and a few other keys are kept by the desktop and never reach
programs.)<P>
<PRE>
Characters    their character code ('A' is 65)
Return   13        Escape  27        Backspace  8
Delete  127        Space   32        Home      30
Tab     &amp;18A       Copy    &amp;18B
Left    &amp;18C       Right   &amp;18D
Down    &amp;18E       Up      &amp;18F
Page Down &amp;19E     Page Up &amp;19F
F1 to F9  &amp;181 to &amp;189     F10, F11  &amp;1CA, &amp;1CB
Shift adds &amp;10 and Ctrl adds &amp;20 to Tab, Copy, the
arrows, Page Up/Down and the function keys.
</PRE>
In JavaScript, write <CODE>&amp;18F</CODE> as <CODE>0x18f</CODE>. F12 is kept by the desktop.<P>

<A NAME=S8></A>
<H2>Menus</H2>
<PRE>
const m = new Menu('Title', [
  { text: 'Info', submenu: infoWindow },        a box
  { text: 'Colour', submenu: otherMenu },       a submenu
  { text: 'Grid', ticked: grid,                 a tick
    action: () =&gt; { grid = !grid; } },
  { text: 'Clear', shaded: true },              greyed out
  { text: 'Save', submenu: box, dotted: true }, line below
  { text: 'Quit', action: () =&gt; task.quit() },
]);
wimp.menus.openAt(m, ev, { task });   open at a click   (10)
colourMenu('Colour', () =&gt; colour, (n) =&gt; { colour = n; })
</PRE>
<CODE>ticked</CODE> and <CODE>shaded</CODE> can be functions, which are called each time the menu opens.
A window created with a <CODE>menu</CODE> property opens that menu itself on a Menu click.<P>

<A NAME=S9></A>
<H2>The icon bar</H2>
<PRE>
task.addIconbarIcon({
  sprite: '!snake',          a sprite from the Wimp's pool
  text: 'Doodle',            text under it (optional)
  onClick: (ev) =&gt; { ... },  Select or Adjust clicked
  menu: makeMenu,            a Menu, or a function making one
  onDataLoad: (ev) =&gt; { ... },   files dropped on it
});
</PRE>
The Wimp's own sprites include <CODE>application</CODE>, <CODE>file_fff</CODE> (Text) and
<CODE>file_f81</CODE> (JSScript). <CODE>*IconSprites</CODE> adds an application's own.<P>

<A NAME=S10></A>
<H2>Dialogue boxes</H2>
<PRE>
infoBox(task, { name, purpose, author, version })     (10)
    an "About this program" box, for an Info submenu
await query({ task, message: 'Discard changes?',
              buttons: ['Discard', 'Cancel'] })
    the text of the button clicked (null if closed)
task.reportError('Something went wrong')
saveAs({ task, filename: 'Doodle', filetype: 0xfff,    (12)
         getData: async () =&gt; text,
         onSaved: (path) =&gt; { ... } })
    a Save box: use it as a submenu, or box.openCentred()
</PRE>

<A NAME=S11></A>
<H2>Messages</H2>
Programs are sent messages by the desktop. Answer one with <CODE>task.onMessage(name, fn)</CODE>; return
<CODE>true</CODE> to claim it.<P>
<PRE>
Quit       the Task Manager wants you to quit: task.quit()
PreQuit    about to shut down: msg.object() stops it (to ask
           about unsaved work first)
DataOpen   a file was double-clicked: msg.path, msg.filetype
</PRE>

<A NAME=S12></A>
<H2>Files</H2>
Full file names look like <CODE>ADFS::HardDisc4.$.MyProgs.Hello</CODE>: <CODE>.</CODE> separates the
directories, <CODE>$</CODE> is the root directory of a disc. The RAM disc is
<CODE>RAM::RamDisc0.$</CODE>. Names don't care about capitals.<P>
<PRE>
vfs.exists(name)                 true if it exists          (12)
vfs.stat(name)                   about it: .name, .type ('file'
                                 or 'dir'), .filetype, .size,
                                 .date ... (null if not found)
vfs.list(dir)                    an array of those, for each
                                 object in a directory
await vfs.readText(name)         a file's contents, as a string
await vfs.readFile(name)         the same, as bytes
vfs.writeFile(name, text, { filetype: 0xfff })   save a file
vfs.mkdir(name)                  make a directory
vfs.delete(name)                 delete
vfs.rename(from, to)             rename or move on one disc
await vfs.copy(from, to)         copy
vfs.parent(name)   vfs.leaf(name)   the directory, the leaf
</PRE>
If something goes wrong - a file that doesn't exist, say - these give the usual RISC OS error, such as
<CODE>File 'Notes' not found</CODE>.<P>
<H3>Some file types</H3>
<PRE>
&amp;FFF  Text        &amp;FFB  BASIC      &amp;F81  JSScript
&amp;FEB  Obey        &amp;FFD  Data       &amp;FF9  Sprite
&amp;AFF  DrawFile    &amp;C85  JPEG       &amp;B60  PNG
&amp;F75  JSON        &amp;DFE  CSV        &amp;FAF  HTML
</PRE>

